WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.251
Reading code is like reading a sentence in another language. You do not need to understand every word. You just spot the giveaway words and symbols. A comment is the easiest: it starts with a hash sign, and the computer ignores it.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:34.243
Operators come in three families. Arithmetic operators do maths, like plus and times. Relational operators compare two values, like greater than or equal to. Logical operators join tests: and, or, not.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:51.372
Each construct has a keyword that gives it away. If starts selection. For and while start iteration. Def defines a subprogram. Count equals zero initialises a variable, and input takes user input.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:06.283
First, name the marker you are looking for. Then scan the code from the top and stop at the first match. Finally, answer in the form asked: the operator itself, the line text, or the line number.

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:18.161
Look at line four of this program. The greater than or equal sign and the less than or equal sign are relational. The word and joins them, so and is the logical operator.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:30.429
Here is a real question from Paper 2 in June 2024. Give the logical operator used in the code. It is worth one mark.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:39.098
Search the code for and, or or not. Line eleven reads: if count is greater than ten and count is less than twenty.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:47.291
Greater than and less than are relational operators, so they are not the answer. The word joining the two conditions is and.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:55.176
So the answer is simply the word and. One mark. This explanation is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:03.883
The most common mistake is giving a relational operator, such as greater than, when a logical operator is asked for.

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:12.885
Another mistake is giving a line number when the question asks for the operator, or skipping blank lines when you count. Give exactly what is asked.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:26.694
Now you try. Using this code, give the line number where iteration starts, a logical operator, and a relational operator. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:41.463
Iteration starts at line three, the while line. The logical operator is or, on line five. A relational operator is less than on line three, or double equals on line five.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:50.650
Remember the method: name the marker, scan from the top, and answer in the form asked. Now try the practice questions for this type.
